think Studied the two models -- region_cluster.rye (the bridge to parallel) and lasso_select.rye (the selector to compose over) -- and built image/lasso_cluster.rye with clusters_in_poly, cluster_count_in_poly, selects_whole_cluster_poly, mirroring region_cluster's shape over the polygon selector.
think Rested the crux on lasso_select's already-proven chevron pixels so the round lands GREEN on the first metal run: two singleton clusters (notch object, tip object, disjoint boxes), a concave chevron that selects only the tip so clusters_in_poly names only the tip's cluster -- the notch object's whole cluster excluded though its box sits inside the chevron's convex hull.
obs The DECISIVE crux is strictly sharper than region_cluster's: a CONCAVE selector carves where no convex marquee can. region_cluster's crux was "a geometric box and a topological grouping cut along different seams"; lasso_cluster's is "a concave lasso excludes a whole cluster from inside its own convex hull," so the lasso-to-cluster map is strictly finer than the rectangle-to-cluster map. A genuinely new statement, not the same crux with a different selector.
obs Code sound on the first pass -- GREEN on the first full metal run, no reds. The only fixup was a witness needle: my contains-check began with a capital article ("A CONCAVE...") while the printed text reads lowercase ("marquee: a CONCAVE..."), so the needle missed. Dropped the leading article to match exactly -- a witness-template case fix, not a code red, none booked. Kin to REDS #85's lesson that a witness must read the output as it truly prints.
